3. Report Scanner — Fast Extraction and Clear Report Details
Improve the existing Report Scanner so users can upload medical reports and receive extracted information quickly and accurately.
User workflow
The user selects or uploads a PDF, image, or supported medical report.
Validate the file and display upload progress immediately.
Extract text directly from digital PDFs when possible.
Use OCR for scanned PDFs and images.
Process only the necessary pages or sections instead of unnecessarily sending an entire long document through an expensive AI workflow.
Parse the extracted content into structured fields.
Display the extracted information in the frontend as soon as reliable results are available.
Allow the user to review, correct, and save the extracted data.
Required report details
Display the following when available in the uploaded report:
Patient's full name.
Age or date of birth, if present.
Sex, if explicitly stated.
Report ID or accession number.
Hospital, diagnostic centre, or laboratory name.
Referring doctor's name.
Report date and test date.
Test names and test categories.
Measured values and units.
Reference ranges printed on the report.
Abnormal results, with high or low indicators where appropriate.
Report impression or conclusion.
Reported findings and possible concerns.
Missing, unreadable, or uncertain fields.
Source page or text evidence for extracted information.
Clearly distinguish between the patient's name, referring doctor, reporting doctor, and laboratory details. Never infer an identity from missing information.
Problem and findings summary
Provide a clear, beginner-friendly explanation of:
What the report measures.
Which values are outside the stated reference ranges.
What the report's written impression says.
What the findings may mean in general terms.
Which points should be discussed with a qualified doctor.
Whether the document contains unreadable or incomplete information.
Do not automatically diagnose a disease from a single laboratory value. Do not confuse an abnormal measurement with a confirmed medical condition.
Speed and reliability requirements
Avoid unnecessary sequential AI calls.
Use direct text extraction before OCR where appropriate.
Process independent pages or sections in parallel with concurrency limits.
Use structured output schemas for extraction.
Cache safe, non-sensitive processing results only where appropriate.
Add request timeouts, cancellation, retries for transient errors, and upload-size limits.
Use background jobs for lengthy reports and show actual processing progress.
Display extracted sections incrementally when practical.
Prevent duplicate processing when the same upload request is retried.
Do not show a fake progress percentage or mark an incomplete report as complete.
Show a clear processing error and retry option if extraction fails.
Report identity and data protection
Treat all uploaded report information as unverified until reviewed. Extracted names and report details must not be represented as independently verified identity.
Store reports privately, enforce user ownership, and allow users to view, download, and delete their own reports. Do not send reports to an external AI provider without the required privacy disclosure and appropriate consent.
4. AI Copilot — Five Fully Supported Languages
Implement a multilingual conversational AI Copilot that supports:
Telugu — తెలుగు
Hindi — हिन्दी
English
Tamil — தமிழ்
Kannada — ಕನ್ನಡ
Language selection
Add a clear language selector to the Copilot.
Allow users to change language at any time.
Persist the user's language preference.
Automatically detect the language of a message when possible.
Allow manual correction when language detection is wrong.
Keep the conversation in the selected language unless the user requests otherwise.
Use appropriate Unicode fonts and rendering for all five languages.
All five languages must be implemented throughout the actual AI workflow, not just in the UI labels.
Natural AI-human conversation
Implement a conversational experience with this workflow:
The user types or speaks a question.
The application sends the message to the backend.
The backend sends the user's question and relevant conversation history to the configured LLM.
The AI generates a response in the selected language.
The application displays the response and optionally speaks it aloud.
The user asks a follow-up question.
The AI uses relevant previous messages to maintain context.
The conversation continues naturally until the user ends or starts a new chat.
Example:
User in Telugu: "నా రిపోర్ట్‌లో హీమోగ్లోబిన్ తక్కువగా ఉంది. దీని అర్థం ఏమిటి?"
AI in Telugu: Explain what the report actually says, what information is missing, possible general interpretations, and when professional medical advice is appropriate.
User: "దీనికి నేను ఏం చేయాలి?"
AI: Provide cautious, context-appropriate general guidance, explain when clinical review is needed, and avoid prescribing treatment without appropriate medical oversight.
Voice conversation
Add microphone controls.
Support recording, stop, cancel, transcript preview, and submission.
Use a configured speech-to-text service that supports the selected language.
Provide optional spoken AI responses using a text-to-speech service supporting that language.
Allow the user to interrupt spoken output, pause playback, or switch back to text.
Preserve conversation context across voice and text messages.
Display the transcript and the generated response in the conversation history.
Handle microphone permission denial, noisy audio, unsupported languages, network errors, and service failures gracefully.
If the user chooses Telugu, Hindi, English, Tamil, or Kannada, verify that the speech services and configured LLM can support the selected language. Do not silently substitute a different language.
Save voice transcripts and recordings only according to the user's consent and retention preferences.
5. Emergency Call 108
Replace the previous Call 112 button in the primary emergency interface with a prominently displayed Call 108 button, as requested.
Requirements:
Use an actual telephone link such as tel:108.
On compatible mobile devices, tapping the button should invoke the native phone dialer.
Keep the action accessible without requiring login, report upload, ABHA linking, or AI processing.
Show a clear fallback on desktop or unsupported devices.
Do not automatically call emergency services.
Do not claim a call has connected merely because the telephone link was opened.
Do not build a fake call interface that claims a real connection.
Important India-specific configuration:
Verify the intended deployment location and current official emergency-service routing.
Configure 108 for the intended ambulance service and region.
Clearly explain that 112 is India's integrated emergency number and may be the appropriate fallback where 108 is unavailable.
If 108 does not operate in a target region, explain the limitation and present the appropriate verified emergency contact.
Do not claim that 108 works in every region or that the app can confirm call completion.
For life-threatening symptoms, make emergency calling immediately accessible. Do not delay the action with a chatbot conversation or form.
