Professional contact information.
Date of birth or age, where necessary.
Medical qualification.
Medical college or awarding institution.
Qualification year.
State medical council or applicable registration authority.
Medical registration number.
Years of professional experience.
Current professional status, including retired status where applicable.
Specialization, if any.
Practice history and relevant credentials.
Consultation languages.
Consultation type: online or in person.
Consultation fee and availability.
Supporting documents for verification.
Do not expose sensitive identity documents publicly.
Verification workflow
Implement these distinct states:
Draft.
Submitted.
Pending Verification.
Verified.
Rejected.
More Information Required.
Suspended.
Verification process
Validate required form fields.
Validate file type and size.
Securely store submitted documents.
Check registration credentials against an authoritative medical registration source when an authorized integration or documented verification procedure is available.
Record the verification source, timestamp, result, and reviewer where appropriate.
Support authorized manual review if automated verification is unavailable.
Notify the doctor about application status.
Keep audit logs of verification decisions.
IMPORTANT: Do not classify a doctor as fake merely because an external verification service is unavailable or a search returns no result.
Only mark a credential as verified when sufficient authoritative evidence supports that decision. Otherwise use Pending Verification or Unable to Verify.
Do not describe a person as a fraudulent doctor without appropriate evidence and review.
Display verified professional credentials and verification status to patients without exposing private documents.
Retired doctors
Allow eligible retired doctors to register their professional history and credentials.
Require appropriate confirmation of their current registration or authorization status and eligibility to practise, according to applicable rules, before enabling consultations.
Retirement history or years of experience alone must not establish current eligibility to practise.
